WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.470
Whales are the biggest
animals of all time.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:09.470
Heavier than elephants, wooly
mammoths, and even dinosaurs.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.653
But they weren't always
the titans of the sea.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.430
Let's rewind the clock
around 50 million years.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.940
No, you won't find any whales here.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.740
You have to go ashore.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.003
Meet Pakicetus, the very first whale.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.500
Life on earth spent millions
of years clawing its way

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.330
out of the oceans.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.830
But whales took all that effort
and threw it out the window.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.970
From 50 - 40 million years ago,

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.090
they traded in their
four legs for flippers.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.240
In fact, some whales today still have

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.060
leftover bones of hind legs.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.650
Once submerged, their weight under gravity

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.150
no longer mattered.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:57.070
So they could theoretically
grow to enormous proportions.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:58.113
And they did.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.250
Today, a blue whale is
10,000 times more massive

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.230
than the Pakicetus was.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.400
But this transformation wasn't as gradual

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.970
as you might think.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.920
In fact over the next
37 million years or so,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.420
whales grew increasingly diverse,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.500
but their size remained small.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.610
For example, one of the first whales

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.490
with baleen instead of
teeth was only 18 feet long,

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.760
making them easy prey for
predators, like giant sharks.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.480
It wasn't until around
three million years ago

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.300
that an ice age tipped the
scales in the whales' favor.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.050
Ocean temperatures and currents shifted,

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.770
sparking concentrated swarms of plankton,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.810
and plankton-seeking krill.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.350
It was an all-you-can-eat
buffet for the baleen whales,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.640
who grew larger as a result.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.450
And the larger they became,
the farther they could travel

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.433
in search of more food to grow even more.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.960
You can probably see where this is going.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.130
Three million years later,
humpbacks, for example,

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:04.430
have one of the longest
migrations of any mammal on earth

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.910
traveling over 5,000 miles each year.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:11.210
As a result, modern whales are
the largest they've ever been

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:12.690
in history.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.950
Take the biggest of the
bunch, the blue whale.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.510
It weighs more than a Boeing 757,

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.270
has a belly button the size of a plate.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.050
And its network of blood vessels,

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.800
if you laid them out in a line,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.730
could stretch from Pluto
to the sun and back,

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.640
over two and a half times.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.730
In fact, the largest
blue whales are so huge

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.480
that scientists think they
may have hit a physical limit.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.860
When they open their wide mouths to feed,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.320
they engulf enough water to
fill a large living room.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.760
So it can take as long as 10
seconds to close them again.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.670
Scientists estimate once
a whale is 110 feet long,

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.490
it can't close its mouth fast
enough before prey escapes.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.840
So it's possible we're living
amongst the largest animal

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.240
that will ever exist.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.263
Lucky for us, they mostly just eat krill.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.660
This was made in large
part thanks to Nick Pyenson

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.763
and the information in his
new book, "Spying on Whales."

